# Require review from any member of the reviewers team.
# Currently: heskew (Nathan) and tps-sherlock (Sherlock).
# One approval from either satisfies branch protection.
* @tpsdev-ai/reviewers

# The release trust root (#1890). The tagger, its workflow, the version checker
# and the advisory allowlist all execute from the default branch, so changing
# what the tagger does is a repo-admin decision — never something a release PR
# carries (condition 7b keeps a release PR inside the release surface).
/.github/workflows/release-*.yml @heskew
/scripts/release-auto-tag.mjs @heskew
/scripts/check-version-sync.mjs @heskew
/.github/release-auto-tag-advisories.json @heskew

# flair#1928 slice 1: the machinery the privileged promote job will EXECUTE (the
# canary block it emits, the registry hashers/skew check and the lockstep source)
# and the promote workflows. A non-release PR must not be able to land an
# exfiltrating version of any of these on main, so they join the trust root.
/scripts/ci/canary-verdict.sh @heskew
/scripts/ci/registry-tarball-sha256.mjs @heskew
/scripts/ci/registry-latest-skew.mjs @heskew
/scripts/ci/lockstep-packages.mjs @heskew
/.github/workflows/canary.yml @heskew
/.github/workflows/release-promote*.yml @heskew

# …and the ownership map itself. Without this line the catch-all rule above
# makes the reviewers team the owner of CODEOWNERS, so a collaborator with merge
# access and that team's approval could delete the entries just above, then
# change the tagger in a later pull request. Changing who owns the trust root
# takes the same human as changing the trust root.
/.github/CODEOWNERS @heskew
